      <!-- ******************** WHY A FOUNDATION MODEL ********************** -->
      <section data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Why a foundation model for detector data?</h1>

        <p style="margin-top:30px">Today, essentially every reconstruction task gets its
          <hl>own</hl> network, trained on its <hl>own</hl> labels, for <hl>one</hl> detector.</p>

        <div class="fragment" style="margin-top:40px">
          <h2>The foundation model recipe</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>Pre-train one large model <hl2>self-supervised</hl2> on huge amounts of
              <em>unlabeled</em> data</li>
            <li>Freeze it. Attach a small, cheap, <hl2>task-specific adapter</hl2></li>
            <li>Repeat step 2 for every new task, with very little labeled data</li>
          </ol>
          <img class="paperfig" style="width:64%;margin-top:15px"
            src="images/fm4npp/intro_fig_white.png" />
        </div>

        <div class="fragment" style="margin-top:25px">
          We have exactly the right raw material: simulation and detector data are
          <hl3>abundant and unlabeled</hl3>; truth labels are expensive. But detector data is
          <em>not</em> text and <em>not</em> images &mdash; it is
          <hl>sparse, 3D, unordered, and variable-length</hl>.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ******************** FM4NPP: WHAT IT IS ********************** -->
      <section data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>FM4NPP: a scaling foundation model for NPP</h1>
        <div class="columnsf">
          <div class="columnnb">
            <h2>The data</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>sPHENIX TPC: 48 layers, $\sim$160k channels, 41.6M voxels</li>
              <li><hl>11M+</hl> simulated $p+p$ events at $\sqrt{s}=200$ GeV</li>
              <li>Event = set of spacepoints $(\mathcal{E}, x, y, z)$</li>
            </ul>
            <h2 style="margin-top:40px">The trick: make it a sequence</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><hl2>Hierarchical raster scan</hl2>: bin in $(r,\eta,\phi)$, order the
                bins outward, order points inside</li>
              <li>Backbone: stack of causal <hl2>Mamba-2</hl2> blocks &mdash; linear in
                sequence length, not quadratic</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="columnnb">
            <h2>Self-supervision without labels</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>Naive "next token" would just learn the serialization</li>
              <li>Instead: <hl>k-next-nearest-neighbor</hl> prediction &mdash; from hit
                $s_i$, predict the $k$ closest hits at <em>larger radius</em></li>
              <li>$\mathcal{N}_c(s_i)=\{s_j \mid r_j \gt r_i\}$, &nbsp; $k=10$</li>
              <li>Loss is plain coordinate MSE. No truth tracks anywhere.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="fragment" style="margin-top:40px">
              <hl3>This teaches the model how a trajectory continues.</hl3>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="columnnb" style="flex:0.55">
            <img class="paperfig" style="width:100%" src="images/fm4npp/preprocessing_pretrain.png" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="src">Figures: D. Park, S. Li, Y. Huang et al., arXiv:2508.14087</div>
      </section>

      <!-- ******************** FM4NPP: WHAT IT SHOWED ********************** -->
      <section data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>FM4NPP: it scales, and it generalizes</h1>

        <h2>Neural scaling: clean power laws in model size, data and compute</h2>
        <img class="paperfig" style="width:66%" src="images/fm4npp/neural_scaling.png" />

        <div class="columnsf dense" style="margin-top:15px">
          <div class="columnnb" style="flex:1.6">
            <h2>Frozen backbone + tiny adapters beat specialists</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><hl2>Track finding</hl2>: beats Exa.TrkX and EggNet. Against the official
                sPHENIX CA+Kalman chain (high-$p_T$, long tracks):
                <hl>99.6% vs 94.6%</hl> efficiency</li>
              <li><hl2>Particle ID</hl2>: best accuracy, recall and precision of all baselines</li>
              <li><hl2>Noise tagging</hl2>: matches OneFormer3D &mdash; with a <hl>0.74M</hl>
                parameter head against its <hl>45M</hl></li>
            </ul>
            <p class="fragment">The representation is <hl3>task-agnostic</hl3>: one linear map
              specializes it per task.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="columnnb" style="flex:0.7">
            <img class="paperfig" style="width:100%" src="images/fm4npp/background.png" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="src">Figures: D. Park, S. Li, Y. Huang et al., arXiv:2508.14087</div>
      </section>

      <!-- ******************** THE GENESIS PROJECTS ********************** -->
